The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in Somalia, in particular resolutions 2719 (2023), 2748 (2024), 2753 (2024), and 2767 (2024),

Reaffirming its full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence, and unity of Somalia,

Underscoring that the Federal Government of Somalia has the primary responsibility for ensuring security, stability, and the protection of civilians within its territory, and welcoming its continued commitment to lead national security operations and progressively assume full security responsibilities,

Commending the pivotal contribution to peace and stability in Somalia made by the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) and the African Union Support and Stabilisation Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM), and paying tribute to the bravery and sacrifices of personnel who have served in these missions,

Recognising the essential role played by the United Nations Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS) in providing operational and logistical support to Somali security forces and the African Union mission,

Expressing grave concern that the terrorist group Al-Shabaab continues to pose a serious threat to the peace, security, and stability of Somalia and the wider region, and condemning in the strongest terms all terrorist attacks, illicit trafficking of weapons, and destabilising activities conducted by Al-Shabaab and affiliates linked to ISIL/Da’esh,

Welcoming the ongoing implementation of the Somali Security Development Plan (SSDP) and the National Security Architecture, and stressing the importance of sustained, coordinated, and predictable international support aligned with Somali-led priorities,

Taking note of the progress made by the African Union and the United Nations in implementing the Joint AU-UN Roadmap on the Operationalisation of Security Council Resolution 2719 (2023), and emphasising the necessity of establishing sustainable, predictable, and accountable financing mechanisms for AU-led peace support operations authorised by the Security Council,

Welcoming the political progress achieved in Somalia, including ongoing efforts towards inclusive constitutional reform and the preparation for direct, one-person-one-vote elections, and urging constructive dialogue between the Federal Government of Somalia and Federal Member States,

Expressing deep concern at the ongoing humanitarian crisis in Somalia, exacerbated by conflict, displacement, and climate-related shocks, and calling upon all parties to facilitate full, safe, rapid, and unhindered humanitarian access in accordance with international humanitarian law,

Determining that the situation in Somalia continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to authorise the African Union Support and Stabilisation Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM) for a further period of 12 months, until 31 December 2026, to carry out the strategic objectives and tasks set out in resolution 2767 (2024);

2. Authorises the member states of the African Union to deploy up to the authorised troop and police ceilings established for AUSSOM, in consultation with the Federal Government of Somalia, to support the phased transfer of security responsibilities to the Somali security forces;

3. Calls upon AUSSOM and the Somali National Army to enhance joint planning and operational coordination in offensive operations against Al-Shabaab, to consolidate territorial control, secure main supply routes, and create enabling conditions for civilian-led stabilisation and governance;

4. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide logistical support through UNSOS, on a reimbursable and cost-recovery basis where applicable, to AUSSOM and to endorsed Somali security forces engaged in joint operations with AUSSOM, within the parameters authorised in relevant Council resolutions;

5. Reaffirms its commitment to the operationalisation of resolution 2719 (2023) for AUSSOM, takes note of the consultations between the United Nations Secretariat, the African Union Commission, the Federal Government of Somalia, and international partners regarding mission support and financing options, and calls on all stakeholders to finalise the necessary technical, oversight, financial accountability, and compliance arrangements;

6. Urges the African Union and troop- and police-contributing countries to ensure that all AUSSOM operations comply strictly with international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and to maintain robust mechanisms for the prevention of, and response to, civilian harm;

7. Encourages the Federal Government of Somalia to accelerate the generation, training, and integration of capable, accountable, and affordable national security forces, in line with the SSDP, to enable the timely and sustainable drawdown and eventual exit of international forces;

8. Calls upon the Federal Government of Somalia and the Federal Member States to strengthen political cooperation, advance inclusive national reconciliation, complete the constitutional review process, and enact agreed electoral frameworks to facilitate universal franchise elections;

9. Condemns the continued illicit proliferation of small arms and light weapons and the illicit financial flows that sustain Al-Shabaab, and calls upon all Member States to strictly adhere to their obligations under the relevant United Nations sanctions regimes;

10. Expresses grave concern over ongoing threats to maritime security off the coast of Somalia, including illicit trafficking and piracy, and urges regional and international naval forces to maintain coordination with the Federal Government of Somalia to ensure freedom of navigation and maritime safety;

11. Requests the Secretary-General, in close consultation with the African Union Commission and the Federal Government of Somalia, to provide a joint report on the implementation of this resolution, including an assessment of AUSSOM's operations, the transition benchmarks, and the status of 2719 financing modalities, every six months, with the first report submitted no later than 15 June 2026;

12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

