9. Condemns the continued illicit proliferation of small arms and light weapons and the illicit financial flows that sustain Al-Shabaab, and calls upon all Member States to strictly adhere to their obligations under the relevant United Nations sanctions regimes;

10. Expresses grave concern over ongoing threats to maritime security off the coast of Somalia, including illicit trafficking, piracy, and armed robbery at sea, underlines the importance of protecting commercial shipping and freedom of navigation, commends the contribution of multinational naval operations, including EU NAVFOR Operation Atalanta, urges regional and international naval forces to maintain close coordination with the Federal Government of Somalia, in full respect of Somalia's sovereignty and territorial integrity, to ensure freedom of navigation and maritime safety in full accordance with international law, in particular the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), and condemns the illicit maritime smuggling of weapons, ammunition, and components to Al-Shabaab and ISIL-Somalia from regional conflict zones, which directly threatens freedom of navigation and regional stability;

11. Requests the Secretary-General, in close consultation with the African Union Commission and the Federal Government of Somalia, to provide a joint report on the implementation of this resolution, including an assessment of AUSSOM's operations, the transition benchmarks, and the status of 2719 financing modalities, every 120 days, alongside regular quarterly oral briefings to the Council, with the first written report submitted no later than 15 April 2026;

12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

